No unit refuses with "no checker answer for the pattern", so no pattern the
lowering now demands an answer for goes unvisited by the checker. The honest
fix is the reconstruction, byte for byte, with the derivation deleted.

While run 3 (`9350219`, the map-list span) ran self-host, the desk-* four --
`whodunit.sh` was the tool for it, and it was lying. It truncates a unit at a
definition boundary; on a RESOLVED unit that drops chapters the header still
cites, and `bundle::load` re-resolved them against the ambient `CODEX_ROOT` on
another branch while the oracle read the truncated bytes. `CODEXC_RAW=1`
(`90cd2cf`) returns the bytes unread, matching what the oracle sees on stdin;
both prefix tools export it. This is the same apples-to-oranges the corpus
provenance guards against, one layer down, and it is why `mintprofile` read
zero expr-types on our side for every prefix.

## desk-* parked: an environment interaction, not a WebSocket bug (SCOPE)

The `desk-*` four (+32 next-row-id, nothing else) were the counter gate's
cleanest remaining family, so I dug. Chapter-boundary bisection of desk-hover
(75,369 lines, 218 chapters, a 60-line DeskHoverTest plus its whole resolved
GUI closure) pointed at WebSocket. It is a red herring: `encode-web-socket`
(1,414 lines, the SAME WebSocket chapter with its own deps) AGREES exactly,
4918 rows both sides. So the 32 rows appear only when WebSocket is checked
inside desk-hover's 200-chapter environment -- an interaction, likely a name
resolving to a different arity in the larger scope (the chapter-collision
shape), not a local bug.

Method notes banked: chapter-boundary bisection is SOUND (deps resolve
backward, a truncated prefix breaks no reference); mid-chapter / per-definition
truncation is NOT (defs reference each other in any order, the oracle refuses
the truncated prefix, and a quick bisector reads that refusal as a divergence
and narrows to a false site -- `whodunit.sh`'s `decide` nudge exists for this).
A divergence that vanishes in isolation was never localized there.

**PARKED by Steve's direction:** do not chase corner-case language coverage in
the Rust front end until U57 validation and the U58 candidate are further
along -- safari has not been run against this pin yet. This is U55-era row
accounting, exposed only because the counter gate became readable this week,
not a U57 regression. The fix, when it is worth making, starts from a small
constructed reproducer.

